 ## Architecture
 
@@ -23,4 +23,12 @@ Resolve the immutable source revision before rendering. The cache key includes c
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses 2Q so that a long sequential screening cannot evict the smaller set of comparison sheets editors revisit. A newly rendered sheet enters a probationary FIFO queue; a sheet is promoted into a protected LRU queue only when it is accessed again while still in the probationary queue. Sheets seen once and never revisited leave the probationary queue without ever reaching the protected queue. When admission requires eviction, the worker evicts from the probationary queue first and draws from the protected queue only once the probationary queue is empty, keeping one-off screening sheets from displacing reused comparison sheets.
+
+The split of the 12 GiB budget between the two queues is a tunable parameter of the worker. All queues, counters, and promotion state are private to the preview worker; the `GET /internal/previews/...` outcomes, the four-job render queue, and the 12 GiB bound are the same under any replacement policy.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q (probationary FIFO queue plus protected LRU queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | The workload mixes long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small working set. 2Q confines first-use sheets to the probationary queue, so a screening scan cannot evict reused comparison sheets held in the protected queue. LRU lets a long scan displace that working set; aged frequency counts add counter and aging maintenance and keep a prior project's popular clips warm after editors switch projects. 2Q costs a second queue and a tunable size split, accepted to protect comparison sheets during big screenings. The choice is internal to the preview worker: the review API, render queue, and disk budget are unchanged under any option, so nothing outside the worker depends on it. |
